{0 The API on one example}

[wire] describes a binary format once, as an OCaml value, and derives from
that value everything that reads and writes the bytes: a decoder and an
encoder, in-place views of untrusted buffers, folds over many records,
generated OCaml, OxCaml and Rust, and a verified C validator. This page shows
the model on one running example: the CCSDS space packet primary header, six
bytes that a ground station routes by its [apid].

{1:codec A codec describes the bytes once}

{@ocaml[
open Wire

type packet = {
  version : int;
  type_ : int;
  sec_hdr : int;
  apid : int;
  seq_flags : int;
  seq_count : int;
  data_len : UInt16.t;
}

let apid = Codec.(Field.v "APID" (bits ~width:11 U16be) $ fun p -> p.apid)

let data_length =
  Codec.(Field.v "DataLength" uint16be $ fun p -> p.data_len)

let packet =
  Codec.v "SpacePacket"
    (fun version type_ sec_hdr apid seq_flags seq_count data_len ->
      { version; type_; sec_hdr; apid; seq_flags; seq_count; data_len })
    Codec.
      [
        (Field.v "Version" (bits ~width:3 U16be) $ fun p -> p.version);
        (Field.v "Type" (bits ~width:1 U16be) $ fun p -> p.type_);
        (Field.v "SecHdrFlag" (bits ~width:1 U16be) $ fun p -> p.sec_hdr);
        apid;
        (Field.v "SeqFlags" (bits ~width:2 U16be) $ fun p -> p.seq_flags);
        (Field.v "SeqCount" (bits ~width:14 U16be) $ fun p -> p.seq_count);
        data_length;
      ]
]}

A field states its width on the wire ([uint16be] is two bytes,
[bits ~width:11 U16be] eleven bits of a big-endian 16-bit word) and its OCaml
type. [Codec.( $ )] binds it to the record, and the constructor takes the
fields in their order. Everything below is derived from [packet]. A shape
that no verified backend can express is refused here, by {!Wire.Codec.v},
with [Invalid_argument].

{1:values Values: decode and encode copy the whole record}

{@ocaml[
let print buf =
  match Codec.decode packet buf ~off:0 with
  | Ok p -> Fmt.pr "apid %d@." p.apid
  | Error e -> Fmt.pr "refused: %a@." Error.pp e
]}

[buf] comes from an attacker, so no byte makes a decoder raise: bad bytes are
an [Error], never an exception. [Invalid_argument] means a misuse by the
program: a negative offset, or a record that [Codec.encode packet p buf ~off]
would write as bytes {!Wire.Codec.decode} refuses.

{1:views Views: check once, then read and write in place}

A router that reads one field per packet should not copy the other six. A
view reads the field where it lies.

{@ocaml[
module View = Codec.View

let apid_f = View.field packet apid

let apid_of buf ~off =
  let (Buf.Any b) = Buf.v buf in
  let c = View.check packet b ~off in
  if View.is_ok c then Some (View.read b (View.get c) apid_f) else None
]}

The definition [apid] says what the field is and exists before the codec,
since {!Wire.Codec.v} takes it. The field [apid_f] says where it lies in
[packet], which only [Codec.v] can compute: [Codec.v] resolves each
definition it receives (a constant offset or how to compute one, and the
field's reader and writer), and {!Wire.Codec.View.val-field} hands that out,
plain data read with no lookup. A definition in two codecs is resolved in
each, and a field that is only decoded or encoded needs just the definition.

{!Wire.Codec.View.val-check} runs every check of the codec once. It never raises
and allocates nothing: its answer is an immediate integer, and so is the view.
{!Wire.Codec.View.is_ok} says whether the record passed and
{!Wire.Codec.View.get} makes the answer a view, as [Result.is_ok] and
[Option.get] do; [get] of a refused check raises [Invalid_argument], since
[is_ok] said so. A view reads a field with no check of its own, and
[View.set b v apid_f x] writes it in place. {!Wire.Buf.v} names the buffer
with a type ['b], which the check and the view carry, so a view is read and
written only in the buffer it was checked in. Why a record was refused is out
of line: [Codec.validate packet buf ~off] answers its [Error].

{1:folds Folds: views over many records}

{@ocaml[
let route_of_apid apid = apid land 3

let routes buf =
  View.fold packet
    Codec.[ apid ]
    buf ~init:(Array.make 4 0)
    ~f:(fun counts apid ->
      let r = route_of_apid apid in
      counts.(r) <- counts.(r) + 1;
      counts)
]}

A fold checks each record of [buf], as [check] does, and hands the fields it
names to its visitor [f], positionally and in the order of the list, as
[Codec.v] hands them to the constructor. It resolves the definitions once,
when it is applied, and reads the fields during the check. By default the
records lie back to back; [~stride] places them in fixed slots and
[~offs ~lens] takes an index of frames, as a capture file lists them. Back to
back, a refused record stops the fold with [Error (e, acc)], since its length
cannot be trusted to find the next record. In slots or an index, [~invalid]
is called on a refused record and the fold goes on; without [~invalid] it
stops there too.

{1:forms Two forms, same concepts}

Everything so far is the {e interpreted} form: [wire] alone, nothing
generated, fields addressed by the codec's resolution of the definitions it
was built from. It is for scripts, the toplevel, codecs built at run time and
the reference oracle. The {e generated} form is a module [Wire_ocaml] (OCaml
or OxCaml) or [Wire_rust] writes from the same codec, in which a field is a
function named after it. It is for hot loops and other languages, and the
OCaml module links [wire.runtime] only.

{v
(* gen.ml *)
let () = Wire_ocaml.(main [ v ~fold:[ "apid" ] Space.packet_codec ])

(* dune *)
(rule
 (targets space_packet.ml space_packet.mli)
 (action (run ./gen.exe %{targets})))
v}

{@ocaml skip[
let apid_of buf ~off =
  let (Buf.Any b) = Buf.v buf in
  let c = Space_packet.check b ~off in
  if Space_packet.is_ok c then Some (Space_packet.apid b (Space_packet.get c))
  else None

let routes buf =
  Space_packet.fold buf ~init:(Array.make 4 0) ~f:(fun counts apid ->
      let r = route_of_apid apid in
      counts.(r) <- counts.(r) + 1;
      counts)
]}

Switching is a one-to-one rename:

{table
  {tr {th Interpreted ([wire])} {th Generated ([Space_packet])}}
  {tr {td [View.field packet apid]} {td none: the module has it built in}}
  {tr {td [View.check packet b ~off]} {td [Space_packet.check b ~off]}}
  {tr {td [View.is_ok c], [View.get c]}
      {td [Space_packet.is_ok c], [Space_packet.get c]}}
  {tr {td [View.read b v apid_f]} {td [Space_packet.apid b v]}}
  {tr {td [View.set b v apid_f x]} {td [Space_packet.set_apid b v x]}}
  {tr {td [View.fold packet Codec.[ apid ] buf ~init ~f]}
      {td [Space_packet.fold buf ~init ~f], its fields declared in [gen.ml]}}
  {tr {td [Codec.decode packet buf ~off], [Codec.validate packet buf ~off]}
      {td [Space_packet.decode buf ~off], [Space_packet.validate buf ~off]}}
}

A generated module has its own record type and its fields are functions,
where the interpreted form's are values; it meets the signatures of
[Wire_ocaml] ([FIXED], [FOLD]), which a test ascribes it to. The differential
tests prove that both forms answer the same on the same bytes. A generated
module has at most one fold: two field sets over one codec are two modules
([v ~name:"space_packet_route" ~fold:[ ... ]]).

{2:same_shape The same shape as the generated module}

A program can give the interpreted form the generated module's functions,
with the same arguments and labels, so that one line switches between the
two:

{@ocaml[
module P = struct
  let apid_f = View.field packet apid
  let check b ~off = View.check packet b ~off
  let is_ok = View.is_ok
  let get = View.get
  let apid b v = View.read b v apid_f
  let set_apid b v x = View.set b v apid_f x

  let fold ?stride ?offs ?lens ?invalid buf ~init ~f =
    View.fold ?stride ?offs ?lens ?invalid packet Codec.[ apid ] buf ~init ~f
end

(* [module P = Space_packet] runs it on the generated form. *)
let apid_of buf ~off =
  let (Buf.Any b) = Buf.v buf in
  let c = P.check b ~off in
  if P.is_ok c then Some (P.apid b (P.get c)) else None
]}

The resolution is data ({!Wire.Codec.View.val-field}), not a function: a reader
made by partial application falls under the value restriction, its buffer
brand ['b] becomes weak, and each [Buf.Any] unpacking has a fresh ['b]. The
eta-expanded functions keep ['b] polymorphic and only interpret the field
resolved once. [wire] does not generate such a module, since the interpreted
form needs no generation.

{1:nested A nested codec}

The same concepts hold when a field holds a codec. [Packet_stack.codec],
called [eth] here, is an Ethernet header, then an IPv4 header when
[ether_type] is [0x0800], then a TCP header when the packet is unfragmented
TCP. Its IPv4 and TCP codecs are called [stack_ipv4] and [stack_tcp], and its
bound fields [eth_ipv4], [ipv4_protocol], [ipv4_tcp] and [tcp_dst_port] are
called [ipv4], [protocol], [tcp] and [dst_port]. [ipv4] and [tcp] are
optional fields that hold a codec: {!Wire.Codec.opt_layer} makes each a
layer.

One check validates the frame and every layer present in it. Reaching a
layer gives that codec's check, whose [is_ok] says whether the layer is
present, and makes no check again. {!Wire.Codec.View.val-layer} resolves a layer
in its codec, as [View.field] resolves any other field.

{@ocaml skip[
let ipv4_l = Codec.opt_layer ipv4
let ipv4_f = View.layer eth ipv4_l
let protocol_f = View.field stack_ipv4 protocol

let retag b ~off =
  let c = View.check eth b ~off in
  if View.is_ok c then begin
    let c4 = View.sub b (View.get c) ipv4_f in
    if View.is_ok c4 then begin
      let v4 = View.get c4 in
      if View.read b v4 protocol_f = UInt8.v 6 then
        View.set b v4 protocol_f (UInt8.v 17)
    end
  end
]}

The module [Wire_ocaml.v ~name:"eth" eth] writes has one submodule per codec
a field holds, named after the codec and written once however many fields
hold it: [StackIPv4] is [Eth.Stack_ipv4].

{@ocaml skip[
let retag b ~off =
  let c = Eth.check b ~off ~len:(Bytes.length (b :> bytes) - off) in
  if Eth.is_ok c then begin
    let c4 = Eth.ipv4 b (Eth.get c) in
    if Eth.Stack_ipv4.is_ok c4 then begin
      let v4 = Eth.Stack_ipv4.get c4 in
      if Eth.Stack_ipv4.protocol b v4 = UInt8.v 6 then
        Eth.Stack_ipv4.set_protocol b v4 (UInt8.v 17)
    end
  end
]}

A fold names a nested field by its path: [l / f] is the field [f] under the
layer [l], and [l // l'] the layer [l'] under [l]. A field under an optional
layer reaches [f] as an [option], [None] where the layer is absent, and one
option however deep the path:

{@ocaml skip[
let tcp_l = Codec.opt_layer tcp

let count buf ~offs ~lens =
  View.fold eth
    Codec.[ ipv4_l / protocol; ipv4_l // tcp_l / dst_port ]
    buf ~offs ~lens ~init:0
    ~f:(fun n proto dport ->
      match (proto, dport) with
      | Some _, Some d when UInt16.to_int d = 80 -> n + 1
      | _ -> n)
]}

The generated module takes the same paths, spelled with its accessor names:
[Wire_ocaml.v ~name:"eth" ~fold:[ "ipv4.protocol"; "ipv4.tcp.dst_port" ] eth]
writes [Eth.fold buf ~offs ~lens ~init ~f].

{1:backends Verified backends}

Each codec projects to the verified parser technology that can express it,
and is checked there: EverParse 3D for binary layouts.

{v
(* gen_3d.ml *)
let () =
  Wire_3d.main ~mode:`Ffi ~package:"space" [ Wire_3d.pack Space.packet_codec ]
v}

This writes [SpacePacket.3d], runs EverParse on it for a verified C
validator, and [Wire_stubs] binds that validator to OCaml. On a refusal the C
tier answers the [Error] {!Wire.Codec.validate} answers, so every tier gives
one error for the same bytes.
